\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces The DreamerV3 world model and actor-critic (Hafner et al., 2023). Observations are encoded into a compact latent state whose evolution a sequence model predicts from actions; the actor and critic then learn on trajectories \emph {imagined} inside the model rather than in the real environment. Predictions are conditioned on the agent's \emph {native} action, the body-specific conditioning signal this thesis replaces with a latent action.}}{7}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces V-JEPA~2 multistage training (Assran et al., 2025). Left: self-supervised pretraining on internet-scale video with a mask-prediction objective in \emph {representation} space (the JEPA objective), which favours motion-relevant features. Right: the encoder is then frozen and an action-conditioned predictor (V-JEPA~2-AC) is learned on top. This project reuses only the frozen encoder as a per-frame visual front-end (Section 3.3).}}{9}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Overview of UniSkill (Kim et al., 2025). (a) An Inverse Skill Dynamics model and a Forward Skill Dynamics model are jointly trained on diverse video to encode a body-independent skill representation; (b) a skill-conditioned policy is trained on robot data; (c) skills extracted from a human-video prompt are executed on the robot to reproduce the behaviour. The inverse/forward pairing matches this project's ITM/FTM (Section 3.4); the difference is that UniSkill \emph {aligns} its representation across bodies by training on cross-embodiment data, whereas this project tests whether such invariance \emph {emerges} without any alignment objective.}}{11}{figure.caption.8}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.4}{\ignorespaces LAC-WM architecture (Huang et al., 2026), the pipeline this thesis adapts. During pretraining an inverse model encodes an observation pair into a unified latent action that conditions a forward model, and a motion decoder maps the latent back to each embodiment's motor labels; a finetuning-stage action projector lets the world model also condition on an unseen body's raw actions. The paper's inverse/forward \emph {dynamics} models are what this project instantiates on visual embeddings and renames the Inverse/Forward \emph {Transition} Model (Section 3.4).}}{12}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.5}{\ignorespaces The QWM framework (Danesh et al., 2026). A Physical Morphology Encoder derives a static embedding from each robot's USD design file and conditions the world model on it (left); a policy is then learned entirely in imagination for a new morphology (middle) and deployed by injecting that morphology's embedding (right). The morphology is \emph {supplied} from a design file, exactly the privileged input this thesis removes by inferring behaviour from external vision.}}{13}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.6}{\ignorespaces The L3P framework (Zheng et al., 2025). A shared latent policy backbone is wrapped by a per-robot observation encoder (raw sensing $\to $ shared latent space) and a per-robot latent-action decoder (latent action $\to $ robot-specific motor command), with recovery modules enforcing latent-space consistency. This shared-latent-with-body-specific-heads structure parallels this project's latent action plus Motion Decoder, but L3P operates on proprioception and must fit an encoder and decoder per robot.}}{14}{figure.caption.11}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces System pipeline. A frozen V-JEPA2 encoder maps each RGB frame to an embedding $e_t$; the Inverse Transition Model infers the latent action $z_t$ from $(e_t, e_{t+1})$; the Forward Transition Model predicts $\hat {e}_{t+1}$ from $(e_t, z_t)$ (loss $\mathcal {L}_{\text {recon}}$); and the Motion Decoder maps $z_t$ back to a body-specific joint command $\hat {a}_t$ (loss $\mathcal {L}_{\text {motion}}$).}}{22}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces A single timestep with a bit-identical joint command across the three bodies. The left-middle foot carries 5.7~N on the long body and 9.3~N on the short body but only 0.3~N on the medium body, where it is still airborne. The command cannot distinguish the bodies; the difference exists only in the resulting physical state.}}{26}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Why per-body commands are needed. Left: identical joint angles place the foot in different positions on a long versus a short leg. Right: inverse kinematics inverts the constraint, so a shared foot target is reached by different joint angles per body, which is the body-specific command the Motion Decoder must learn to produce.}}{26}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Left: top-down body trajectories (five episodes per morphology) under an identical command sequence, aligned to a common start and heading and drawn at true aspect ratio. Right: per-episode path length and net displacement with mean $\pm $ standard deviation; the three morphologies do not overlap.}}{29}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.5}{\ignorespaces Morphology is strongly and ordinally encoded in the frozen embedding. (a) A supervised linear probe reaches ${\sim }100$ percent and holds under episode-grouped cross-validation. (b) Unsupervised PCA, given no labels, orders the three bodies by leg length along its leading component. (c) A UMAP projection, shown for illustration only. Leg length and recording session are confounded in this pilot.}}{30}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.6}{\ignorespaces Foot-contact behaviour is decodable within a body (mean macro-F1 0.84) but collapses across bodies (mean 0.16), and a shuffled-label control sits at the chance floor. The behaviour information is present but entangled with body shape, the signal the latent action must preserve while making it body-independent.}}{30}{figure.caption.20}%
